.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd September 6, 2026
.Dt SYS-INIT-CHECK-NATIVE-EVIDENCE 1
.Os sys-init experimental
.Sh NAME
.Nm sys-init-check-native-evidence
.Nd audit recorded native experiment artifacts offline
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm python3 scripts/check-native-evidence.py
.Nm python3 scripts/check-native-evidence.py
.Fl Fl help
.Nm python3 scripts/check-native-evidence.py
.Fl Fl version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This is a source-checkout developer command.
The manual title does not name an installed executable.
Run with Python 3.10 or newer from the repository root.
The command finds its catalog relative to the script location.
It checks the catalog schema and uniqueness, retained manifests,
producer/consumer
artifact correspondence, required cases and exact success or rejection
markers.
It reads existing evidence and never launches QEMU or executes a native image.
A passing audit does not qualify current native source changes.
.Pp
The checker writes no evidence or output directory.
The catalog controls the retained bundles in scope; not every directory under
docs/evidence is necessarily part of this catalog.
Use the aggregate command to retain logs and apply its 60-second gate
watchdog.
The standalone audit has no separate elapsed-time or output cap.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Fl help
Print usage and exit without performing the check.
.It Fl Fl version
Print the command version and exit.
This is not a published sys-init release identifier.
.El
.Pp
There are no ordinary operands, repair modes or output-directory options.
.Sh OUTPUT
Success prints JSON with schema sys-init-native-evidence-audit-v1,
result pass, catalog digest, per-bundle counts, total recorded cases and
native set to not-run on standard output.
Caught integrity, marker, schema and file errors print a diagnostic prefixed
by
native evidence audit: on standard error, without a failure JSON object.
Unlike the quality-record checker, this diagnostic is not escaped by the
checker.
.Pp
A missing or mismatched artifact requires investigation of the exact retained
bundle and catalog identity.
Preserve failed attempts and historical manifests.
Do not treat a generic smoke failure as the required semantic rejection or
change expected markers simply to make an audit pass.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
There are no command-specific environment configuration variables.
Python uses its ordinary host environment.
The command does not consult mutable sibling repositories or fetch artifacts.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa docs/native-coverage.json
Catalog of selected immutable bundles, expected cases and manifest identities.
.It Pa docs/evidence/
Retained artifacts and raw logs referenced by the catalog.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
All cataloged evidence checks passed; also help and version.
.It 1
A caught integrity, schema, marker, type or filesystem error.
.It 64
Invalid arguments, with diagnostics on standard error.
.El
.Pp
Unexpected interpreter failures or abrupt termination can produce a nonzero
exit without a normal result.
An absent result is not success.
.Sh EXAMPLES
From the sys-init repository root with prerequisites already provisioned:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
python3 scripts/check-native-evidence.py --help
python3 scripts/check-native-evidence.py
.Ed
.Pp
The first invocation prints usage and exits zero.
The second prints the success JSON and exits zero only if its scoped checks
pass.
To exercise argument rejection without changing inputs:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
python3 scripts/check-native-evidence.py --unknown
.Ed
.Pp
This prints diagnostics on standard error and exits 64.
No root privileges are required by these examples.
Use python3 scripts/check-all.py to retain the normal check and its logs.
.Sh SEE ALSO
Repository references: docs/NATIVE-COVERAGE.md and docs/native-coverage.json,
docs/DEVELOPMENT-CHECKS.md and docs/DOCUMENTATION-INVENTORY.md.
Render this source with the host mandoc tool for offline terminal reading.
.Sh CAVEATS
Independent technical and reader review, installed-target lookup and release
approval remain separate.
This check does not establish runtime conformance.
.Pp
Decision 0126 validates the complete catalog before bundle reads.
Catalog JSON is limited to 4 MiB and 32 nesting levels;
exact fields, supported schemas, bounded collections and unique names apply.
The reported catalog digest identifies the bytes parsed.
Receipt validation and filesystem snapshot guarantees remain separate.
See docs/NATIVE-EVIDENCE-RECORDS.md for exact field and size rules.
.Pp
Decision 0127 requires ordinary catalog and manifest files with bounded reads
and descriptor identity checks.
Manifest JSON is limited to 4 MiB and 10,000 entries, with strict names/digests
and the catalog JSON parsing rules.
The same captured manifest bytes supply both pin verification and parsing.
Payload read limits, receipt validation and final consistency remain separate.
.Pp
Decision 0128 hashes ordinary payload files in chunks of at most 64 KiB,
with a 64 MiB per-file ceiling and descriptor identity checks.
This bounds digest reads only; JSON, serial and envelope content reads remain
separate operations, without an atomic snapshot guarantee.
.Pp
Decision 0129 also bounds JSON, serial, envelope and helper-image content reads
to 4 MiB ordinary files, with component and descriptor identity checks.
Text retains host-default decoding and universal newline conversion.
Hashing and semantic reads still occur separately; strict receipt schemas and
hash-to-content identity remain open.
.Pp
Decision 0130 verifies each captured semantic buffer against the pinned manifest
before decoding or parsing it.
Receipt-correspondence digest reads must match the same manifest.
Later filesystem changes and strict receipt schemas remain separate concerns;
this is not an atomic tree snapshot.
.Pp
Decision 0131 requires complete bundle file membership and rejects symlink
or special entries, with a 20,000-entry traversal limit including directories.
Membership and manifest bytes are rechecked around hashing;
each bundle is reverified after semantic checks and again before CLI success.
Catalog bytes must remain identical to the parsed capture.
These sequential comparisons do not establish an atomic filesystem view.
.Pp
Decision 0132 parses verified receipt/report JSON as UTF-8 object roots,
with the 4 MiB and depth-32 limits.
Duplicate keys, non-finite literals and numeric overflow to infinity reject.
Finite numbers are preserved; complete receipt field validation remains open.
Serial text continues to use host-default decoding.
.Pp
Decision 0133 validates consumer report, case and command fields before linked
receipt access, including exact fields, digest types, booleans and integer
exits.
Reports allow at most 1,000 cases and 3,004 commands; command argv allows
at most
1,000 strings of 4,096 characters each, without ASCII controls or DEL.
Only argv elements may be empty; init marker, context and expected_panic may
be null.
Worker pins must be paired and present for service-worker cases.
Producer and worker receipt field validation remains separate work.
.Pp
Decision 0134 validates consumed producer summaries/reports, service-worker
receipts and I/O supervisor build receipts before using their linked fields.
Exact fields, digest maps, command argv, tool records and integer program-header
rows are checked; the three retained producer summary variants remain supported.
Recorded commands remain data.
SDK recursion and additional artifact correspondence remain separate work.
.Pp
Decision 0135 compares I/O supervisor hashes with retained supervisor.raw files
and requires worker SDK, tool-lock and libc digest fields to match input-map
entries already verified against retained files.
Init consumer ISO artifacts are not retained; their hashes remain structural
claims rather than independently verified artifact identities.
.Pp
Decision 0136 checks exact AENFD001 layout, bounded image and supervisor sizes,
reconstructed native-envelope hashes and embedded supervisor correspondence.
Reconstructed bytes are neither written nor executed.
SDK receipt semantics and unavailable historical ISOs remain separate limits.
.Pp
Decision 0137 validates direct and derived SDK receipt fields at fixed retained
paths, including nested artifacts, headers, patches and input identities.
Derived receipts follow one fixed SDK input and require the direct format there.
Patch snippets allow tab, CR and LF within 65,536 characters; other text keeps
its existing single-line bounds.
Recorded patch, tool and ELF claims are structurally checked, not re-executed.
.Pp
Decision 0138 compares derived SDK input maps and startup/envelope hashes with
retained files, and checks the embedded native image against the worker ELF.
Unavailable direct SDK artifacts remain explicit evidence limits.
.Pp
Decision 0139 checks recorded producer init-case counts and producer/worker
SDK, libc and tool-lock consistency when a service worker is consumed.
Normalized summaries may omit case counts.
Missing producer artifacts and source-archive member correspondence remain
explicit limits.
.Pp
Decision 0140 compares source-archive members with the full summary input map.
Gzip expansion is capped at 32 MiB, with at most 10,000 regular nonsparse files
and 4 MiB per member; no filesystem extraction occurs.
Duplicate, escaping, special, missing and contradictory members reject.
Producer source-map omissions remain distinct from summary-map verification.
.Pp
Decision 0141 compares producer source maps with archive-verified summary
inputs.
Only ten historical metadata omissions are allowed, scoped to exact manifest
pins, normalized schema, names and digests; new or repinned evidence gets no
inherited exception.
Conflicting present entries always reject.
